export type CreatePublicCloudProjectComputeInstanceError = BadRequest | Conflict | OvhOpError;
/** Create an instance ### Create an instance Provisions a new instance from the supplied `targetSpec`. Creation is **asynchronous**: the resource starts in `CREATING` and becomes `READY` once the backend has booted it. Poll `GET .../instance/{instanceId}` until `resourceStatus` is `READY` and `currentTasks` is empty. ### Create-time-only fields (immutable afterward) Several fields can be set **only at creation** and can never be changed later: * `location` -- the region (and optional availability zone) the instance is deployed in. * `sshKeyName` -- the SSH key injected into the instance at boot. * `group` -- **placement / instance-group membership**. It binds the instance to an existing instance group (Nova server group) for `AFFINITY` / `ANTI_AFFINITY` scheduling. Server-group membership is fixed by OpenStack at boot and cannot be added, removed, or changed afterward -- there is no group `PUT`. ### User data (cloud-init) `targetSpec.userData` is a base64-encoded (strict standard encoding) cloud-init payload handed to the instance at boot through the OpenStack metadata service / config drive, and interpreted by cloud-init inside the guest image. The encoded string must be at most 65535 bytes. It is **write-only**: the value is never returned by `GET`/`LIST`. It is also not persisted or stored by the Public Cloud API: the payload is handed to the compute service, which makes it available to the instance via the metadata service; only a non-reversible fingerprint of the value is retained, for change detection. Unlike the create-time-only fields above, `userData` can be changed later via `PUT` -- see the `PUT` route for the destructive semantics of doing so. ### Declarative `volumes` `targetSpec.volumes` is a **declarative list** of block-volume references attached to the instance. Supply the volumes to attach at boot; on a subsequent `PUT` the list is diffed to attach or detach volumes (see the `PUT` route). There is no imperative attach/detach endpoint. ### Declarative `shares` `targetSpec.shares` is a **declarative list** of filesystem (Manila share) attachments. Each entry references a share plus an optional per-entry `accessLevel` (defaults to `READ_WRITE`). At creation, each attach creates a Manila IP access rule for the instance's interface on the share's network -- the instance must have an interface on that network. As with volumes, the list is reconciled by diffing on `PUT`. ### Declarative `networks` and public IPs `targetSpec.networks` is a **declarative list** of network interfaces. Each entry is `{id?, subnetId?, ip?, autoAssignPublicIp?}`, and the fields you set decide what it means: * `{"autoAssignPublicIp": true}` -- a public (Ext-Net) interface with a **platform-assigned** public IP. **At most one** entry may set it. * `{"ip": "..."}` **without** a network `id` -- a public IP the project **already owns**: either an **existing additional IP** (also known as a failover IP), or an **Ext-Net IP of the project** in the instance's region. Any other address is rejected. Such an entry implicitly attaches the public (Ext-Net) interface. **Several** are allowed, and they may coexist with an `autoAssignPublicIp` entry. * `{"id": "...", "subnetId": "..."}` -- a private interface whose fixed address is assigned by IPAM. * `{"id": "...", "subnetId": "...", "ip": "..."}` -- a private interface where `ip` **pins the fixed address** when it falls inside the subnet CIDR, and otherwise designates an **existing floating IP** to associate with that interface (the floating IP must already exist). Any entry that sets `autoAssignPublicIp` or carries a public `ip` requires the optional `instance/usePublicIP` IAM action, including floating IP associations. In `currentState.networks` the shape is different: there is one entry per network plus at most one id-less entry for the public (Ext-Net) interface, and **every** address is reported through that entry's `addresses` list -- there is no scalar `ip`. Each address carries a `type`: `FIXED` for an address configured on the interface itself (a private fixed address, whether IPAM-assigned or pinned, and the Ext-Net address), `FLOATING` for a floating IP associated with the interface, and `ADDITIONAL` for an additional IP routed to the public interface (additional IPs no longer form their own entry). `autoAssignPublicIp` is never echoed back, because the backend cannot distinguish a platform-assigned address from a pinned one. */
